		{
			"id": "d_p0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "# Data preprocessing: a shape search can use, per source\n**Sub-problem of** semantic search **step 12** (the \"sub-problem\" arrow): the same goal, finding the right document for semantic questions, approached from the data side (16 Nov).\n\n**Why:** the 41 semantic questions we still fail (brainstorm 006) fail in two ways:\n- near-twin documents from one template, told apart only by details (customer, numbers, dates);\n- the answer is a side detail of a document about something else.\n\nToday every document is cleaned and cut into chunks the same way, whatever its source. Each source has its own structure (tickets, threads, emails, transcripts), and we could use it.\n\nOne step per source, starting with Linear.",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 700,
			"color": "6"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 1 · Linear: tickets with a description (16 Nov)\n**Why first:** worst failure rate. 6 of its 10 semantic questions fail (60%).\n- 35,308 tickets, 6.9% of the corpus; 58 of the 500 questions expect one.\n- ✓ The parser finds a **description in 99.96%** (13 tickets are free text).\n- Other fields: acceptance criteria 75%, comments 76%, plus ~20 optional sections (notes 25%, rollout plan 23%, tasks 16%, next steps 2%…).\n- Description: median 782 chars, about **18% of the ticket** (ticket median 5,041; p90 4,457 vs 7,011).",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 840,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 488,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s1a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### Where the answers are (58 Linear questions)\nThe ticket section sharing the most words with the gold answer (a rough heuristic):\n- description: 25 (43%)\n- acceptance criteria: 12\n- other sections (notes, rollout plan, decisions…): 15\n- comments: 6\nSemantic questions only: description 3, acceptance criteria 4, other 3.\nSo the description says what the ticket is about, but holds the answer in fewer than half.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 840,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 446,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s1b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 1a · Idea: search descriptions, open the full ticket\n**Idea:** index only the descriptions, one per ticket. The agent searches them to pick tickets, then fetches the whole ticket when it needs the details.\n- ✓ One clean vector per ticket: no comment or acceptance-criteria noise, no chunks of one ticket competing with each other.\n- ✓ The description is the ticket's own summary, written by the source, so no LLM summary to keep up to date as the corpus grows (the reason step 5 of the semantic map stopped).\n- ✗ Risk: twin families have near-identical descriptions (7 \"benchmark store + comparison UI\" tickets).\n- ✗ Risk: side details (qst_0187's AC7) aren't in the description, so the question has to match the ticket's topic.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 1346,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 638,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next1b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next (free, local)\n- Embed the 35k descriptions only (fine-tuned gte, minutes) and search them with the 58 Linear questions: where does the right ticket rank, against today's chunk search?\n- The 6 failing semantic Linear questions (0187, 0214, 0218, 0240, 0275, 0285): does a description search find the ticket?\n- The agent's side: top-k descriptions with titles, then a \"read ticket\" tool. Measure tokens per question against 5 full documents.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 1346,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 378,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 2 · Slack: 35 channels, one vector per thread (16 Nov)\n**Why:** the biggest source, 285,605 documents (55.8% of the corpus); 6 of its 13 semantic questions fail.\n- Unique threads: 281.6k–283.9k, depending on what counts as a copy (0.6–1% exact duplicates; 7,641 threads share their first message with another).\n- **35 channels hold 96.8%** of threads; the other 9,042 are group chats with no channel. 27 channels have 1,000+ threads; the top 10 hold 63% (incidents 24k, eng-platform 23k, eng-runtime 22k…).\n- The title is the channel name, so about 8,000 threads share each title: it tells search nothing.\n- Each thread is one vector, so a long thread (31+ messages, the top 10%) gets squeezed into one.\nReview page with 50 threads (short / long / random + 5 most similar): `exploration/document_review/`.",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 2124,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 638,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s2a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 2a · Idea: a channel filter for the RAG\n**Idea:** keep the parsed channel as metadata and let retrieval use it: the agent searches within chosen channels (search(query, channel=…)), or channels matching the question get a boost.\n- ✓ Free and exact: the parser already reads the channel of 96.8% of threads.\n- ✓ Shrinks the pool: the largest channel has 24k threads instead of 286k.\n- ✓ Helps when the question implies the channel: qst_0224 asks about \"public documentation\", and its answer is in #docs.\n- ✗ Hurts when the answer is in an unexpected channel: qst_0229 asks about credential signing, and its answer is in #sales. A hard filter makes that a certain miss, so it works better as an agent tool or a soft boost.\n- Same idea across sources: a source filter when the question says \"email thread\", \"call\" or \"ticket\" (brainstorm 006).",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 2124,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 698,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next2a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next\n- Of the 79 questions with a Slack right document, how many name or imply its channel? (local, by hand or with the review page)\n- Can an LLM guess the right channel from the question? (Gemini, paid: ask first)\n- Agent side: a channel argument on the search tool, and a list of the 35 channels in its prompt.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 2124,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 288,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "n0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "# Semantic search failures: only 52.8% correct\nWe are building a RAG system (search + LLM answer) over **EnterpriseRAG-Bench**: 512k synthetic company documents (Slack, Gmail, Jira, Drive, meeting transcripts…) and 500 test questions.\n\nMost question types score 80–100%. **Semantic questions** (125, paraphrased so they share few words with the document) score only 52.8%. Why?",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 412,
			"color": "6"
		},
		{
			"id": "n1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 1 · Diagnose the misses\n**Why:** before fixing anything, find out what goes wrong.\n\n- ✗ **Text fixes.** 115k documents were stored as Python lists; we decoded them and rebuilt the keyword index. Recall stayed at 55.2%: not the cause.\n- **Second judge.** An LLM checks whether the document the answer came from really answers the question. Of 57 wrong answers, 10 are *ambiguous* questions (another document answers too). **46 are real search failures.**\n- **Exact ranks.** Where does the right document land among all 512k? Keyword search (BM25): median rank 446. Vector search (gte embeddings): median 2,334. Both far below the top 5 the LLM reads.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 552,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 554,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision\nThe problem is **finding** the right document, not ranking it.\n\nThe corpus is full of near-duplicates: e.g. five documents about the same GPU deal that differ only in \"8 GPUs vs 48\" or \"1-year vs 3-year term\". Search returns the look-alikes instead.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 552,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 308,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 3 · Proposal: our own schema, one SLM pass (16 Nov)\n**Why:** existing ontologies (Teamwork Graph, APQC, ITIL, GraphRAG…) don't fit our data or show nothing we can use, so we define what to mine ourselves. The near-twins differ by attributes (customer, region, date, numbers), so the schema is entities, not topics.\n**Plan:** a small model (Qwen3.5-2B + LoRA, as in the fact-extraction run) mines every document once.\n- labels for 3k–10k documents, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash: **$1–5** (measured: ≈$1 per 3k compact, ≈$4.80 per 10k)\n- label spot check, Gemini: ~$0.50–4\n- LoRA run on 1 × RTX 4090: **~$1** per run, 70 min (measured $1.01); ~$2–5 with retries\n- mining 512k with vLLM: **~$10–25**, 1–2 days on one 4090 (estimate: ~820M input, ~200M output tokens)\n- **total ~$15–35**, 2–3 days\nFor comparison: DeepSeek on all 512k ≈ $250, Gemini ≈ $1,400.\n✗ Unknown: inference speed. We measured only plain generate, 25 s per document (≈150 days, ≈$1,500 for 512k).\n→ Superseded by step 5: zero-shot extractors first, this LoRA route only as the fallback.",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 2962,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 908,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Before the full pass (avoid a second one)\n- Define the schema from the review notes (Slack page) and the failure patterns (brainstorm 006).\n- Measure vLLM speed on 1,000 documents (~$0.10).\n- Pilot on ~10k documents (~$0.50), check the output on the failing questions.\n- One 512k pass; **store the raw model output**, so new fields are re-parsed, not re-mined.\nA second pass costs only ~$15–25, but 1–2 days of GPU plus a re-index.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 3588,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 386,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_dec3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (16 Nov): mining with an LM is the path\n- **Search already reaches the right topic.** In the 41 failures the documents that beat the right one are on its topic: near-twins from one template (brainstorm 006). What's missing is depth inside the topic: which customer, region, date or number.\n- **That needs attributes the text doesn't state** (\"a healthcare client\" = CytoHealth, \"a payments company\" = Greenfield), which only a language model can mine.\n- Keywords per document are what BM25 already does (tokens, stopwords, stemming), and the fine-tuned gte vector already holds the topic.\n- So: one SLM pass with our own schema (this step), as planned. Rules and NER for the attributes the text states (names, regions, ids, dates).",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 2962,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 566,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "end9b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⛔ Dead end: one score threshold (11 Nov)\nNo reranker score separates real negatives from other right answers inside the grey zone. Replaced by the biggest drop per question (9c).",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 9556,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 200,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_dec8b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (20 Nov): train the SLM\nMined fields earn their cost: field search + agent lift the Slack dev top 5 from 39.0% to 55.5%, and the answer holds the right case in 96.4% of the ambiguity set. Next: [[Train the Slack field extractor SLM]], then [[Update the pipeline with the accepted experiments]] and the final evaluation.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 15558,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 330,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "n3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 2 · Embedding fixes\n**Why:** maybe the vectors are too coarse (one vector per 24,000 characters averages the detail away).\n\n**Test setup (\"candidates\"):** for each of the 46 failed questions, take vector search's own top 50 plus the right document (~51 candidates), and see where the right one ranks among them.\n\n- ✗ **Smaller chunks** (4k / 2k / 1k characters): right document in top 10 in 0–1 of 46.\n- **Another model** (jina-v3, 1k chunks): 9 of 46. Better, but partly because the candidates were picked by gte; also non-commercial licence.\n- ✗ **Late chunking** (each chunk sees the whole document): worse in 27 of 46. It blurs the one detail that matters.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 1310,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 614,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✗ Conclusion\nNot a dilution problem. Text embeddings can't tell near-duplicates apart: the right document and 50 others look equally relevant.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 1310,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 188,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "n5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 3 · Reranker\n**Why:** the reranker (a cross-encoder that reads question + document together) is our most precise scorer, but it only sees the top 50 candidates.\n\n- If the right document were among its candidates, it would put it in the **top 5 in 29 of 46** cases.\n- But each document costs ≈ 65 ms to rerank (50 docs ≈ 3.2 s per question). A shorter 2k-character input is 4.6× faster but loses ~7 points of recall.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 2152,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 404,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision\nDon't just widen the reranker's candidate list (too much compute). The fix has to come **before** it: get the right document into the candidates.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 2152,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 188,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "n7",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 4 · Agents\n**Why:** let an LLM search several times with its own queries instead of one fixed search.\n\n- **Snippet agent:** keyword results come back as short snippets, with a tool to read a full document. Semantic: 52.8% → **60.8%** (cost $7.17 for 125 questions).\n- **Keyword agent + Gemini caching:** caching the conversation start saves money, not time.\n- On **completeness** questions (need several documents): 55% → **70%**.\n\nHelps, but costs per question and still misses the hidden near-duplicates.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 2720,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 524,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5 · Fact extraction\n**Idea:** the near-duplicates differ in *details* (amounts, people, dates, ids). Extract those details from every document into short facts and index them, so search can match on them.\n\nAt 512k documents an API LLM is too expensive, so: label a sample with a big LLM, then teach a **small local model** to do the same.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 3448,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 344,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n9",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5a · Labels and model\n- ✓ **Labels from DeepSeek V4.1 Flash** (Chinese API, ~25× cheaper than Gemini, terms allow training on its outputs): per document an \"about\" line, up to 20 entities and 12 short facts, as JSON. 3,000 training docs + 2,370 test docs, ≈ $7.\n- ✓ **Small model: Qwen3.5-2B**, fine-tuned with **LoRA** (trains a small add-on, 0.75% of the weights) on a rented RTX 4090: 68 min, **$1.01**.\n- Result: valid JSON on 100% of held-out documents; wording close to DeepSeek's.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 3448,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 404,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n10",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5b · Quality check (LLM judge)\n**Why:** facts that are wrong would send search to the wrong document.\n\nGemini reads the document and checks every extracted fact: supported / not stated / contradicted.\n- **DeepSeek labels: 0.9%** of facts wrong, none invented — slips like the wrong person or a merged clause.\n- **Our fine-tuned model: 2.6%** wrong (3× more), worst on long meeting transcripts (7%).\n- Facts keep 50% of what benchmark answers need — they're short by design.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 3448,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 464,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n11",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decisions (8 Nov)\n- DeepSeek labels pass: good enough to train on.\n- Facts are for **search only**; the answer is still read from the full document, so 50% coverage is fine.\n- No 10k label set (API credit nearly used up).",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 3448,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 248,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "n12",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Open (dropped: step 5 stopped)\nIs the fine-tuned model's 2.6% error rate acceptable, or does it need more training data for transcripts?",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 3756,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 220,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "n13",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5c · Retrieval pilot\n**Why:** do the facts actually help search? Same candidates as step 2 (46 failed questions), using DeepSeek's facts.\n\n- ✗ **All facts as one vector per document:** right doc in top 10 in 1 of 46. Averaging 12 facts blurs the key one.\n- **One vector per fact:** 12–14 of 46 (gte or MiniLM), better than text vectors… ✗ but never improves on keyword search, at any weight.\n- **Keyword search (BM25) over the facts**, added to today's search: **2 → 20 of 46** in the top 10.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 4036,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 434,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n14",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5d · Cost\nVector indexes must sit in RAM, so size = server bill.\n\n- **BM25 facts field:** +0.5 GB, **≈ $0** extra.\n- **One vector per fact:** 6.7M vectors, +15–50 GB → **+$175–500 per month** on managed Postgres (today ≈ $175–210).",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 4036,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 314,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "n15",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Leaning\nAdd the facts as a **keyword (BM25) field**. Don't build a per-fact vector index unless a full test shows a gain keyword search can't get.",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 4036,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 188,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "n16",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next (not done: step 5 stopped)\n- **Full-index test:** add the facts field to the real index and run all questions. The candidates favour anything that isn't gte, so check we don't lose questions that work today.\n- **Cheap alternatives:** give the reranker the facts instead of a long chunk; embed facts only for the top candidates at query time; tiny binary vectors (≈ 0.65 GB).",
			"x": 2400,
			"y": 4036,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 370,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "legend",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**How to read**\n🟪 **One map, all problems**, side by side left to right; a labelled \"sub-problem\" arrow links a problem to the step it grew out of.\n⬇ **Down = progress**: the steps in the order we did them.\n➡ **Right = depth**: what a step found, the decision on it, and where the route ends.\n\n**Legend**\n🟪 problem · 🟦 step\n🟩 decision / conclusion\n🟨 open\n\n**Where a route ends**\n🟥 ⛔ **Dead end**: tried, nothing left to explore\n🟧 ⏸ **Stopped**: could work, but not worth the cost (for now)\n\n✓ chosen · ✗ didn't work\n\n**Route groups**\n🟨 ▶ **In progress**: being worked on\n🟥 ⏸ **Not exploring** (dashed border): stopped or rejected\n🟩 ✓ **Accepted**: worked, in use",
			"x": -460,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 340,
			"height": 760
		},
		{
			"id": "end2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⛔ Dead end: nothing left to try\nSmaller chunks, another model and late chunking all failed for the same reason. No embedding variant is left that could separate near-duplicates.\n\n**Reopen only if** a new idea changes *what* gets embedded, not how.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 1310,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "end3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: too expensive\nReranking more candidates would work (top 5 in 29 of 46), but at ≈ 65 ms per document it costs seconds per question.\n\n**Reopen if** a faster reranker appears, or once another step shrinks the candidate list.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 2152,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 280,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "end5c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⛔ Dead end: all facts as one vector\nAveraging ~12 facts into one vector blurs the one fact that matters (right doc in top 10 in 1 of 46). Nothing left to tune.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 4936,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "end5d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: one vector per fact\n6.7M extra vectors, +15–50 GB, **+$175–500 per month**, and it never beat keyword search over the same facts. Not worth the gigabytes or the time (9 Nov).\n\n**Reopen if** a full test shows a gain BM25 can't get.",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 4566,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 310,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "end5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: LLM summaries / facts per document (9 Nov)\n- **Not enough gain:** even on the small pilot (the candidates of 46 failed questions), the summaries didn't help search enough to justify the work.\n- **Doesn't scale:** the corpus keeps growing. Every new document needs a summary, and we can't keep guaranteeing the accuracy of the old ones as the model and data change.\n\nA structured taxonomy (step 6) makes this less likely, but doesn't rule it out.\n\n**Reopen if** buckets alone can't separate the near-duplicates.",
			"x": 3000,
			"y": 4036,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 470,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_t_Sea",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Search models**",
			"x": -1140,
			"y": 840,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 50
		},
		{
			"id": "m_gte",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### gte-modernbert-base · embedder\nSemantic search. Alibaba, **149M** params.\n- **768-dim** vectors; reads up to **8,192 tokens**.\n- We store chunks of ~24,000 characters, so ≈ 1 vector per document (512k vectors).\n- ✗ Can't tell near-duplicates apart.",
			"x": -1140,
			"y": 900,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 278,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_rr",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### gte-reranker-modernbert-base · reranker\nCross-encoder: reads question + document together. **149M** params.\n- Reads **8,192 tokens** (question + document), so it sees whole documents.\n- ≈ **65 ms per document** → 50 candidates ≈ 3.2 s per question.\n- ✓ Top 5 in 29 of 46 failures *if* the right document is among its candidates.",
			"x": -1140,
			"y": 1208,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 376,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_bm25",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### BM25 (bm25s) · keyword search\nNo model: exact word matches, rare words weigh more.\n- ✓ Best single signal on the failures' candidates (top 10 in 31 of 46).\n- ✗ Misses paraphrases (\"step down\" vs \"annealing\").",
			"x": -1140,
			"y": 1614,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 278,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_minilm",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### all-MiniLM-L6-v2 · small embedder\n**22M** params, **384-dim** vectors, reads **256 tokens** (longer text is cut).\n- Tested in 5c for cheap per-fact vectors: about as good as gte on short facts, half the storage.",
			"x": -1140,
			"y": 1922,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 248,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_jina",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### jina-embeddings-v3 · tested only\nWith 1k-character chunks: 9 of 46 in the top 10.\n- ✗ Non-commercial licence.",
			"x": -1140,
			"y": 2200,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 158,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_t_Lan",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Language models**",
			"x": -620,
			"y": 840,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 50
		},
		{
			"id": "m_qwen",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### Qwen3.5-2B + LoRA · our small model\nFine-tuned fact extractor (LoRA r16, 3 epochs).\n- Native context **262k tokens**; trained at **4,096** (longer documents cut).\n- ≈ **12 s per document** on the laptop GPU (RTX 2000 Ada, 8 GB).\n- Training: 68 min, **$1.01** on a rented RTX 4090.\n- Valid JSON 100%; **2.6%** of facts wrong.",
			"x": -620,
			"y": 900,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 338,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_ds",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### DeepSeek V4.1 Flash · label teacher\nWrites the training labels for the small model.\n- ~25× cheaper than Gemini; its terms allow training on outputs.\n- ≈ **$7** for 5,370 documents; **0.9%** of facts wrong.",
			"x": -620,
			"y": 1268,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 248,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "m_gem",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### Gemini 3.8 Flash · judge and agents\nChecks labels and outputs; drives the search agents.\n- **$0.75** per M input tokens, **$3.75** per M output.\n- ≈ $0.011 per document judged; snippet agent $7.17 for 125 questions.",
			"x": -620,
			"y": 1546,
			"width": 500,
			"height": 278,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "bef2ae1146b17ee9",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 7 · Evaluation improvement\n**Idea:** Currently evaluation assumes that we one-shot the relevant document, but in real life we can make agent ask follow up questions. The main idea is to add evaluation on the same questions, but allow agent to ask about the details and have details prepared.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 7616,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 220,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6 · Wider reranker list + facts\n**Why:** two things helped before: the reranker (when it sees the right document) and BM25 over facts. Test them together.\n\nBoth tests are free and local, on the 46 failures.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 5350,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 244,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6a · How deep must the reranker read?\nToday's search over all 512k documents, but kept 1,000 deep instead of 50. Failures where the right document is in the list:\n- 50 (today): **8** of 46\n- 100: 14 · 300: **22** · 1,000: **30**\n\n✗ 16 failures never show up, even at 1,000.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 5350,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 348,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6b · Reranker on the candidates\nRight document among its ~51 candidates, 46 questions. Recall@5 = found in the top 5.\n- ✓ **Reranker on full text:** recall@5 **54%** (25 found, 21 missed), recall@10 72%. 59 ms per document.\n- ✓ **Reranker on facts only:** recall@5 **54%** (25 / 21), recall@10 61%. **11 ms** per document, 5× faster.\n- ✗ **+ BM25 facts:** recall@5 54–59%, fewer at rank 1. Adds little once the reranker runs.\n- BM25 text alone: recall@5 43%.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 5350,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 470,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Conclusions (9 Nov)\n- The bottleneck is **getting into the list**, not ranking inside it.\n- BM25 over facts doesn't add to the reranker.\n- Facts make deep reranking affordable: 300 fact blocks ≈ 3.3 s per question, about today's cost for 50 full documents.\n- Rough estimate: **52.8% → about 61–62%** semantic (not measured).",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 5350,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 328,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6c · End-to-end test (9 Nov)\n**Why:** check the 61–62% estimate with real search instead of the candidates.\nToday's hybrid search, 300 deep, then the reranker over all 300. Only 20% of these documents have facts; the rest use their first 2k characters. 46 failures:\n- In the fused 300: **20** (at 50: 7).\n- ✓ **Full text @300:** recall@5 **20%** (9), recall@10 22%. **~20 s** per question.\n- ✗ **Facts / 2k @300:** recall@5 11% (5), recall@10 20%. ~4.7 s.\n- ✗ Full text @50 (today's depth): recall@5 4% (2), ~3.8 s.\n- ✗ Text + facts (RRF): recall@5 17%.\n- In the list but not top 5: rank 6–139, median 24. A deeper list holds more near-duplicates.\n- None of today's top-5 hits lost. Passing questions not tested.\n→ semantic at most **52.8% → 60.0%** (full text), 56.8% (facts). Estimate was too optimistic.",
			"x": 2400,
			"y": 5350,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 684,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6b_llm",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6b′ · LLM reranker on fact blocks (idea)\n**Idea:** instead of gte-reranker, give an LLM (Gemini 3.8 Flash) the question and all fact blocks and let it return the top 10.\n\n- Fact blocks are short: **~438 tokens** per document vs ~1,500 full text → **~23k tokens** per question for ~53 candidates (~80k full text).\n- ✗ **LLM price:** ~**$0.019** per question on facts ($2.3 per 125 questions, ~$19 per 1,000); full text ~$0.061; 300 fact blocks ~$0.10.\n- ✓ **Dedicated GPU (L4, ~$580/month):** ~$0.00007 per question at full load, **~250× cheaper**; break-even with the LLM at ~1k questions/day. Serverless GPU ~$0.00008 per question.\n- The reranker already runs on a GPU today, so on facts it costs nothing extra; the LLM is a new cost, slower (est. 2–5 s vs 0.3–0.6 s) and its quality is untested.\n(GPU prices approximate, not checked; LLM quality not measured.)",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 6724,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 728,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "end6b_llm",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: LLM reranker not financially viable (9 Nov)\nAn LLM reranker adds ~$0.019 per question on top of a GPU we already pay for, ~250× the GPU's cost per question, with no measured quality gain. It also needs facts for every document (step 5 stopped).\n\n**Reopen if** a test on the 46 failures (~$0.90) shows an LLM ranks near-duplicates clearly better than gte-reranker (recall@5 54%), or LLM prices drop ~10×.",
			"x": 2400,
			"y": 6724,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 386,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "end6c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: deeper reranker list (9 Nov)\n+9 of 125 semantic at best, at ~20 s per question (full text); facts give only +5 and need facts for every document. The snippet agent already reaches 60.8%.\n\n**Reopen if** a routing step shrinks the list so the right document is in a short list, or a much faster reranker appears.",
			"x": 3000,
			"y": 5350,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 356,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6e_next",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next\n- **Routing** for the **26** failures that aren't in the fused 300: keywords / buckets, see the **document growth** map.\n- Check that a deeper list doesn't hurt the questions that pass today.",
			"x": 3000,
			"y": 5766,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 228,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6d_trunc",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6d · Rerank on the first N tokens (9 Nov)\n**Why:** is the facts' 5× speed-up the extraction, or just shorter input? Cut each document to its first N tokens, no model.\nCandidates (recall@5 / @10, ms per doc):\n- full text 54% / 72%, 58.5 ms · facts 54% / 61%, 11.4 ms\n- ✗ first 256: 43% / 50%, 5.9 ms\n- first 512: 52% / 57%, 11.8 ms (≈ facts)\n- ✓ **first 1,024: 59% / 70%, 26 ms**\nEnd to end, 300 deep: first 1,024 = full text (recall@5 20%) at 27 vs 66 ms per doc.",
			"x": 3000,
			"y": 6118,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 458,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s6d_trunc_dec",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (9 Nov)\nFor reranker speed, **cut to the first 1,024 tokens**: full-text quality at ~2.3× the speed (50 docs: ~3.1 s → ~1.3 s), no model, nothing goes stale. Facts are no longer needed for speed. Not switched on yet; use when latency is needed (agents, deeper lists).",
			"x": 3600,
			"y": 6118,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 258,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "abstain_ptr",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Related problem found (9 Nov)\nWhen search misses, the agents answer from a near-duplicate in ~55–60% of cases instead of saying \"I could not find this\" (pipeline: 20%).\n\nTracked as its own problem in this map (the \"related problem\" arrow).",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 7616,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 258,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "s8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8 · What does the embedder add? (10 Nov)\n**Why:** before training anything, measure what gte brings next to BM25.\n\nReplayed gte, BM25 and fused top 50 for all 500 questions (742 right documents):\n- both find it: **54%**\n- BM25 only: **24%**\n- ✗ gte only: **3%**\n- neither: **19%**\n\ngte mostly finds what BM25 already finds; it fails on semantic questions.\n`exploration/retriever_attribution/`",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 8014,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 528,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s8a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8a · Bigger general embedder: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\n**Why:** the usual advice is to try a stronger model before fine-tuning.\nSample estimate (100 questions, 163 right docs, 5k distractors; full re-index would take ~23 h):\n- gte: top 50 49%, with BM25 **128**\n- Qwen: top 50 55%, with BM25 **128**\n- ✗ semantic 9 vs 9 of 27: better only at keyword-like matches BM25 already has.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 8078,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 378,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "end8a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⛔ Dead end: swap in a bigger general embedder\nEqual hybrid result (128 vs 128). Near-duplicates need training on our data, not a bigger model.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 8078,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 186,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "s8b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8b · Who can tell near-duplicates apart? (teacher)\n46 semantic failures, ~53 candidates each, right document in top 5:\n- gte: 0\n- ✓ **gte-reranker: 25** (median rank 4, local, free)\n- gemini-embedding-2: 17 (spreads near-duplicates 2.7× wider, but ranks worse than the reranker in 26 of 46)",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 8604,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 378,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "dec8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (11 Nov)\n**Fine-tune gte with hard negatives**, using the reranker as the teacher. The benchmark stays for evaluation only. (Article 006, decisions 10–11 Nov.)",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 8604,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "s9",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 9 · Picking hard negatives cheaply (11 Nov)\n**Why:** a near-duplicate ranked just below the answer is the best negative, but some \"negatives\" are really other right answers (false negatives).",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 9146,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 228,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s9a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 9a · Reranker + LLM ambiguity judge\nBrainstorm 003. Reranker picks the grey zone, an LLM judges each candidate.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 9210,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 198,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "end9a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: judge too expensive\n~$24 for 2,000 questions with Gemini.\n**Reopen if** the drop rule's false negatives turn out to hurt training.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 9210,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "s9b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 9b · One score threshold\nBrainstorm 004. Is there a reranker score gap where the model stops being sure?\n- ✗ No threshold works inside the grey zone; a fixed 0.02 cut was set by hand from 6 points.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 9556,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 214,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s9c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 9c · Biggest drop per question\nLargest step between neighbouring reranker scores in the top 30; negatives = up to 5 just below it; skip the question if its source is below.\n- Benchmark failures: right doc above the drop 27 of 46.\n- ✓ Training-like sample: **91 of 100**, median 1 above the drop.\n- ✗ Knee detectors (Kneedle, TAA-k) and a small ML model: no better (46 examples too few to learn the line).",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 9918,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 394,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "dec9",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (11 Nov)\nUse the **biggest-drop rule**: free, local, no judge. Literature: knee detection (Vectara, TAA-k), positive-aware filtering (NV-Retriever). Brainstorm 004.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 9918,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 208,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "s10",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 10 · Our own training questions (11 Nov)\n**Why:** keep the benchmark for the final test only, and tune on a dev set so we don't overfit to it.\nBenchmark's own prompts (MIT), one-document types first, same type and source mix. No evaluation document or its 20 nearest neighbours as a source. Brainstorm 005.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 10476,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 348,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s10a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 10a · Generation + mining\n- 3,120 sources, **3,011 valid** questions, **$0.96** (DeepSeek, LangGraph)\n- intra-document: 238 of 347 pass the benchmark's check\n- mining: search + rerank + biggest drop, local, ~3.2 h\n- ✓ kept **train 2,067 / 2,510**, **dev 422 / 501**, 5 negatives each\n- semantic skipped 35%; for 61% of semantic questions the source wasn't in the fused top 50, yet the reranker rescues most → exactly what the embedder must learn\n- review fix: no dev answer as a train negative (and the reverse)",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 10476,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 514,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s11",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 11 · Fine-tune gte with hard negatives (12 Nov)\n**Why:** teach the embedder our near-duplicates, the failure no general model fixed (step 8).\nFull fine-tune of gte-modernbert-base (149M) on 2,067 questions, 5 hard negatives each, cached MNRL, 32 questions per step. Rented RTX 4090 ($0.37/h).\nDev check: rank each dev question's right document among its own ~51 search candidates.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 11130,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 348,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s11a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 11a · Runs (dev, 422 questions)\nTop 10 / MRR / semantic top 10:\n- before: 49% / 0.35 / 23%\n- ✗ laptop, docs cut to 1,024 tokens: 55% / 0.34 / 47% (top 1 drops)\n- full length, lr 2e-5: 73% / 0.50 / 68%\n- lr 1e-5: 78% / 0.56 / 69%\n- ✓ **lr 5e-6, 2 epochs: 82% / 0.56 / 77%**\n- lr 5e-6, 3 epochs: 82% / 0.57 / 72%\n- 64 per step: 78% / 0.58 / 67% (sharper top, fewer found)\nGentle training wins; full-length documents are essential.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 11194,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 454,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "dec11",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (12 Nov)\nUse **lr5e-6-e2**: best top 10 and semantic, tied MRR. The embedder's job is to get the right document into the reranker's list, so top 10 counts most.\nIts vectors go in separate `_768_ft` tables; retrievers `gte-ft-chunk`, `hybrid-ft`, `hybrid-ft-rerank`.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 11194,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 298,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "end11a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: more tuning\n5-epoch curve and a plateau learning-rate schedule left out: the last runs differ by a few questions, and dev is written like train.\n**Reopen if** the benchmark gain is small.",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 11194,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 208,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "s11b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 11b · Real search over the whole corpus (dev)\nCorpus re-embedded on 4 × RTX 4090 (512k chunks, 21 min, ~$2.30). 422 dev questions through production search, base vs fine-tuned:\n- embedder top 50: 61% → **70%** (semantic 35% → 53%)\n- fused top 50: 80% → **84%**\n- ✓ reranked top 10: 80% → **84%** (semantic 49% → **61%**)\n- first place: 70% → 74%\nSmaller than the candidate check: near-duplicates from the whole corpus compete now. The reranker places every listed right document (353/353).",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 11732,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 498,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s11c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 11c · Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B fine-tune\n**Why:** untouched it beats untouched gte on dev (top 10 61% vs 49%, semantic 41% vs 23%).\nSame settings + a task instruction for questions. At 40% of training, MRR 0.54 on 100 dev questions, level with gte.\n- ✗ crashed at 60%: disk full (each Qwen checkpoint ~7 GB).",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 12354,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 348,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "end11c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: Qwen fine-tune (12 Nov)\nOut of Vast credit ($0.60 left). ~$1 to finish (60 GB disk, one checkpoint), ~$5 more to re-embed the corpus.\n**Reopen if** the benchmark shows gte-ft still misses many semantic questions.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 12354,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 238,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "s11d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 11d · Benchmark run (13 Nov)\n**Why:** dev questions are ours; the benchmark's 500 are the real test.\nRun 31, `hybrid-ft-rerank`, all 500 questions, **$4.53**, against the base runs 7, 17, 19–26. Correct answers:\n- ✓ overall 75.4% → **77.6%**\n- ✓ basic 86.3% → **90.9%**\n- ✗ semantic 52.8% → **54.4%** (+5 / −3 questions)\nDev had promised semantic 49% → 61% in the top 10.",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 12786,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 354,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "con11d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✗ Conclusion (13 Nov)\nFine-tuning helps overall but barely moves semantic questions, the failure it was trained for. Why: step 12.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 12786,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 168,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "s12",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 12 · Where do the right documents get lost? (13 Nov)\n**Why:** find out why the fine-tuned gain doesn't reach the benchmark.\nReplayed base and fine-tuned search on the 125 semantic questions; where the right document lands at each stage (local, free). Base → fine-tuned:\n- gte alone, top 50: 38 → **59**\n- fused 50 (the reranker's list): 75 → 80\n- reranked top 5: 69 → 72\n- not in the reranker's list: 50 → 45\n`exploration/embedder_finetune/benchmark_semantic_check.py`",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 13280,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 458,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "con12",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Conclusion (13 Nov)\n- ✓ The embedder did learn: gte alone finds 21 more right documents.\n- ✗ BM25 already had most of them, so fusion adds only 5.\n- ✓ The reranker isn't the limit: it puts ~90% of the right documents it sees in the top 5.\n- Fusion keeps only 50 of ~100 candidates and cut 8 right documents the fine-tuned gte found (its ranks 35–48).",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 13280,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 348,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "s12a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 12a · Rerank the union of both lists (15 Nov)\n**Idea:** each question gets gte top 50 + BM25 top 50, about 97 different documents (only ~3 shared), but fusion keeps 50. Rerank all of them instead. No new search; full text, nothing cut.\nRight document in the final 5, 125 semantic questions (fused 50 → union):\n- base gte: 69 → 73\n- ✓ fine-tuned gte: 72 → **80** (55.2% → **64.0%** against today's production)\n- fine-tuned: gained 10, lost 2; not among the candidates 45 → 31\n- ✗ rerank time per question: 3.3 s → 6.3 s\n`exploration/embedder_finetune/union_rerank_check.py`",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 13688,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 548,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "next12a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next\n- **Full benchmark run** with fine-tuned gte + union (paid, ~$4.50): does 64% hold in real answers, and do other question types lose?\n- **Then speed:** e.g. rerank the first 1,024 tokens (step 6d).\n- **The 31 right documents in neither list:** try HyDE and query rewriting (todo).",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 13688,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 288,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "s12b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 12b · HyDE and query rewriting (dev, 15 Nov)\n**Idea:** let Gemini write a passage that would answer the question (HyDE) and 4 rewritten search queries; search with them too. Fine-tuned gte, 118 semantic dev questions, top 5 (Gemini $0.21):\n- union, question only: 61.0% (97 docs, ~6 s)\n- ✓ + 4 rewrites as extra searches: **69.5%**, ✗ 392 docs, ~24 s rerank\n- + HyDE as extra searches: 65.3% (180 docs); adds nothing on top of rewrites\n- all 12 lists fused, RRF top 100: 67.8% (~6 s)\n- ✗ replacing the question: HyDE in gte 50.0%, a paraphrase 47.5% (the fine-tuned gte matches questions)\nAll 422 dev: union vs fused 50, top 5 82.2% → 83.9%, no type loses.\n`exploration/query_expansion/expansion_check.py`",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 14296,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 608,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s12c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 12c · Merge before the search (dev, 15 Nov)\n**Why:** keep the gain without more candidates. Still one gte + one BM25 search (~96 docs, ~6 s rerank), 118 semantic dev questions, top 5:\n- question / question (union): 61.0%\n- question / keyword rewrite: 63.6%\n- mean(question, rewrites) / question + rewrites as text: 65.3%\n- ✓ **mean(question, HyDE) vectors / keyword rewrite: 66.9%** (+7 questions; ✗ doesn't hold on the benchmark, 12d)\n- ✗ mean of all 5 vectors with HyDE: 62.7%\nOne question = 0.85 points; 1–2 points is noise.\n`exploration/query_expansion/expansion_merge.py`",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 14964,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 518,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "s12d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 12d · Merged query on the benchmark (16 Nov)\n**Why:** dev questions are written like our training ones; check the merges on the benchmark's 125 semantic questions (Gemini $0.22). One gte + one BM25 search, ~95 docs. Top 5, test / dev:\n- question / question (union): 64.0% / 61.0%\n- ✓ **question / question + 4 rewrites appended: 67.2%** / 63.6% (+7 / −3 questions)\n- mean(question, HyDE) / question: 65.6% / 63.6%\n- mean(question, 4 rewrites) / question + rewrites: 65.6% / 65.3%\n- ✗ dev's best, mean(question, HyDE) / keyword rewrite: **60.8%** / 66.9%\n- ✗ question / keyword rewrite: 60.8% / 63.6%\nBM25 alone, right doc in its top 50 (test / dev): question 62.4% / 42.4%, keyword rewrite 52.0% / 49.2%, ✓ question + rewrites **72.0% / 59.3%**.\n`exploration/query_expansion/benchmark_merge_check.py`",
			"x": 600,
			"y": 15606,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 668,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "con12d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (16 Nov)\n- ✓ **Rewrites go to BM25 only, appended to the question; gte keeps the plain question.** It holds on dev and the benchmark.\n- ✗ HyDE and vector means dropped: the LLM doesn't know the answer or our documents, so its guesses add noise to the vector; wins there look like a lucky keyword.\n- BM25 needs only one right \"golden keyword\", and appending keeps the question's own words.\n- ✗ The gain is small: BM25 finds 10–17 points more right documents, but only 3–4 more questions reach the top 5. The reranker loses them among near-duplicates.",
			"x": 1200,
			"y": 15606,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 468,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "next12d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next (brainstorm first)\n- Why does the reranker lose the extra right documents BM25 finds?\n- How many rewrites does BM25 need (keyword one only, or all 4)? Local, free.\n- 24 right documents are still in neither list.\n- Then a full paid benchmark run (~$5–6).",
			"x": 1800,
			"y": 15606,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 258,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5 · Plan: zero-shot attribute mining, Slack first (16 Nov)\n**Why Slack first:** 55.8% of the corpus (285,605 threads) and the least structured source: the title is only the channel, each thread is one vector, and 6 of its 13 semantic questions fail. The other sources have more structure (ticket fields, email headers, CRM records) and are smaller, so a tactic that works on Slack should carry over. *Fight the toughest soldier first, learn how to defeat the whole army.*\n**Papers:** ready schema-driven extractors work zero-shot: GLiNER2 (205M encoder, CPU, entities + classification + structure, Apache 2.0) and NuExtract (0.5–3.8B, JSON templates). Fine-tuned small models still beat zero-shot on extraction (Sub-Billion Super-Frontier: 0.83 vs 0.69 F1; ELMTEX).\nDecision models (Jev, Gemini Decisions, Clef) fit closed-set attributes, but bill per question: ~$35–80 per attribute on 512k.",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 4996,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 668,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_dec5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (16 Nov): the split\n- **Concrete fields** (customer, region, dates, ids, people) → GLiNER2 or NuExtract, zero-shot, from our schema.\n- **Abstracted attributes** (industry, customer size, document purpose) → GLiNER2's classification, zero-shot.\n- **Fine-tune only if that's too weak** (step 3's LoRA, ~$15–35, as the fallback).\n- **Store scores, not just labels:** a filter can be tightened later without another pass. Calibrated probabilities aren't a reason to pick a model.\n- Mined fields become **agent tools or soft filters**, not hard routing.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 4996,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 468,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next (updated 16 Nov)\n1. ✓ Draft Slack schema: 19 span types (topic, person, team, client, region, product, feature, component, model, version, environment, bug, cause, fix, config, metric, identifier, date_time, document) + 11 relations. Decisions and thread kind left out (sentences, not spans). Full table in the task note.\n2. Compare **gliner2-large-v1** (340M, ~512 tokens, spans ≤ 8 words) and **gliner2.5-base-v1** (194M, 4,096 window, any span length). Both fit the laptop GPU (RTX 2000 Ada, 8 GB).\n3. Reference: DeepSeek, exact spans, ≈ $0.0009 a thread (ask first) + a hand check of 30–50.\n4. Score: precision, recall, F1 per field and per relation; micro + macro; exact + partial match; type confusion; threshold sweep per field.\n5. Rollout: 13-thread trial → adjust → ~200 threads → dev pilot (~40k docs) → full Slack pass.\nOnly if classification is needed and weak: decision models (Clef-flash; Jev or Gemini paid).",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 4996,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 678,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s5a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5a · Reading: GLiNER2 in practice (16 Nov)\n**Idea:** check what GLiNER2 can do before we write the Slack schema.\n**How it works:** [GLiNER2 architecture](obsidian://open?vault=Pajari%20vault&file=DS%20Knowledge%20base%2FLLM%2FGLiNER2%20architecture) in the DS knowledge base (diagram, step by step, models).\n**Sources:** [TDS: GliNER2, extracting structured information from text](https://towardsdatascience.com/gliner2-extracting-structured-information-from-text/) (T. Bratanic, Neo4j, 6 Jan 2026; PDF in ~/Documents), [GitHub fastino-ai/GLiNER2](https://github.com/fastino-ai/GLiNER2), [paper 2507.18546](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18546).\n**The test:** `gliner2-large-v1` (340M) on one 322-token Wikipedia paragraph about Ada Lovelace, into Neo4j. A \"vibe check\", not a benchmark.\n**What it offers:**\n- ✓ One schema, one call: entities + relations + classification + structured records (`create_schema()…extract()`). Fits our \"no second pass on 500k\".\n- ✓ Label descriptions steer it (\"Event: historical events, wars, or conflicts\").\n- ✓ `extract_json`: fields as `name::type::description`, type `str` or `list`; runs on CPU.\n- ✓ (repo) confidence per item, choices for closed fields, LoRA adapters of 2–10 MB, Apache 2.0.\n**What went wrong:**\n- ✗ No inference: `gender` null for everyone, though Ada is called \"daughter\".\n- ✗ Records mix people up: in `extract_json`, Babbage and William King became Ada's parents, Lord Byron got \"reformer\" (that's Anne Isabella). The same `parent_of` as a **relation** was right.\n- ✗ Label names matter: `alias` always filled, `same_as` never, with the same description.\n- ✗ A `description` field gives surface phrases, not summaries.\n- ✗ Every field is optional: `name` can be null, so records need validating (Pydantic).\n- Tasks in one call run in parallel: entities don't constrain relations; relations can't restrict head/tail types.\n**What we keep:**\n- only for what the text states (customer, region, dates, ids, people);\n- one value per field rather than records of many linked fields; links as relations;\n- try 2–3 label names per field; validate every record;\n- inferred attributes (industry, size, purpose) go to classification, checked hardest on the ~200 reference labels;\n- long threads: split by message (the long-text helper chunks at 384 tokens).",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 5734,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 1688,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s5b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 5b · Trial: zero-shot GLiNER on 13 Slack threads (16 Nov)\n**Why:** check the cheap extractor before labelling 200 threads.\n**Setup:** 19 span types + 11 relations; DeepSeek labels as the reference ($0.0079); code and report in `exploration/slack_extraction/`.\n- ✓ gliner2-large (384-word chunks) best: micro F1 0.41 exact / 0.48 partial, 0.83 s a thread on the laptop GPU.\n- ✗ gliner2.5-base: 0.33 / 0.40 on chunks, 0.28 / 0.32 on whole threads (the long window lowers recall).\n- ✓ Names: person 0.85, client 0.83, date_time 0.71, version 0.70.\n- ✗ Descriptive fields: feature 0.17, config 0.16, component 0.14, fix 0.09; topic 0.28.\n- ✗ Relations ≈ 0 for every type.\n- Part of the gap is the reference: long DeepSeek phrases, loose types.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 7482,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 668,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_end5b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⛔ Dead end: zero-shot GLiNER for Slack mining (16 Nov)\n- A span picker can't write the fields we need: bugs, causes, fixes, topics and relations.\n- Fine-tuning GLiNER would still only pick short spans.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 7546,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 236,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_dec5b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (16 Nov): a fine-tuned SLM\n- Weighed: fine-tuned GLiNER (≈ $1, still spans), DeepSeek on all Slack (≈ $175 off-peak, $0.0006 a thread, and again per source), an SLM with LoRA (≈ $15–35).\n- **Chosen: the SLM.** It writes phrases and relations and runs cheaply on every source.\n- Reuse the fact-extraction setup (Qwen3.5-2B, `exploration/fact_extraction/train.py`, Vast.ai).",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 7930,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 318,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next5b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next (updated 17 Nov)\n1. ✓ Tighten the DeepSeek prompt (strict tool call, frozen `fc8ae8878988`).\n2. ✓ Hand check on 13 threads and a 30-item smoke test.\n3. ✓ Labels: 25,632 items, reused as training data (step 6).\n4. LoRA the SLM; score it with `score.py` on the held-out 200.",
			"x": 6320,
			"y": 7930,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 320,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6 · Field test: do mined fields find the right thread? (17 Nov)\n**Why:** before training the SLM, check that the fields help search at all. DeepSeek stands in for the SLM: it is the quality ceiling.\n- Set: the 200 Slack semantic dev questions; baseline top 5 **39.0%** (three lists pooled, reranked).\n- The right thread never reaches the reranker for **95 of 200**.",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 8414,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 370,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6a · Labelling run (17 Nov)\n**Why:** fields for every candidate of the 200 questions, the right threads, 1,000 random threads and 1,200 questions; also SLM training data (half A of the questions).\n- ✓ 25,632 of 25,633 items, **$26.54**, one frozen prompt.\n- ✗ Strict mode doesn't enforce enums: off-list areas and labels are repaired in code (568 first failures).\n- Schema: 26 fields, `area` (19 business areas), `concept`, `doc_date`.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 8414,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 420,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6b · Fields as a re-ordering signal (17 Nov)\n**Idea:** reranker score + field match (names, topic, problem, area), weights picked on half A.\n- ✗ Held-out half B: top 5 43 → 45, top 1 31 → 30: noise.\n- ✗ Names matched by embedding: no gain.\n- ✗ Any weight ≥ 0.25 hurts: the cross-encoder already sees what the fields say.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 8958,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 340,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_end6b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⛔ Dead end: fields as a reranker signal\nThe reranker reads question and document together; a few mined words add nothing it doesn't see.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 8958,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 180,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6c · Fields as a search: one shot, then an agent (17 Nov)\n**Idea:** search the 24,432 labelled documents by their fields instead of raw text. Right thread in the first 50, missed / reached questions:\n- Today's gte: 1% / 58%.\n- ✓ One-shot field search: 47% / 84%.\n- Agent v1 (one line per result): 39% / 71%.\n- Agent v2 (+ snippets, picks): 35% / 73%.\n- ✓ **Agent v3 (+ documented areas and profiles): 53% / 86%**; top 10 34% / 70%. $0.38 for 200.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 9446,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 400,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_con6c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Conclusion (17 Nov)\n- ✓ The fields work as a **search**, not as a re-ranker.\n- ✓ The agent only works once its tools are **documented**: what each area holds, how filters match, when to use each profile. Area filter use 11% → 99%.\n- Caveat: a 24k labelled pool, not the full corpus.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 9446,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 320,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6d · Reranker on the agent's candidates (17 Nov)\n**Why:** the end-to-end number. Final top 1 / 5 / 10, all 200 questions:\n- Today's ~128 candidates: 27.0 / **39.0** / 44.5.\n- ✓ Agent v3 top 100: 33.5 / **55.5** / 63.0.\n- Today's + agent top 100: 34.0 / 51.0 / 60.0 (more look-alikes hurt).",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 9994,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 310,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_dec6d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (17 Nov)\nHand the reranker **the agent's top 100 alone**: +16.5 points top 5. The reranker converts ~71% of reached threads, as before; the gain is reach.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 9994,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6e",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6e · Asking the person + source and channel filters (17 Nov)\n**Idea:** the 9 Nov clarification setup (memory notes, 25% with a wrong detail, Gemini plays the person), `ask_user` up to 2 times.\n- ✗ v3 + asking: no gain; replies (\"a chat thread in the release channel\") had no filter to go to.\n- ✓ v4 adds `sources` and `channels` filters: on the 85 questions where it asked, top 10 27% → 39%.\n- ✗ Wrong-detail notes barely gain: a wrong channel hides the thread.\n- Run-to-run noise: 6–10 points at top 10.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 10388,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 430,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s6f",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 6f · How ambiguous are the questions? (17 Nov)\n**Why:** some \"misses\" may be other threads that answer too. Gemini rates each shown document alone (method of `question_ambiguity`), 1,498 ratings.\n- ✓ The right thread answers its question: full 189 / partial 10 / none 1.\n- ✗ **76.5%** of questions: another shown document answers fully, with *different* facts (near-twins).\n- Final top 5 holds an answering document 84.5% (today) / 88.0% (agent), the right thread 39.0% / 55.5%.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 10942,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 460,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_con6f",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✗ Conclusion (17 Nov)\nThe dev set is mostly an ambiguity test. Search already finds *a* fitting thread; choosing *which case the person means* is the gap, and only information from the person can close it.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 10942,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 200,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next\n- **Show the agent where its results disagree** (different fixes, owners, dates) so it asks about what separates them.\n- Repeat runs to measure past the noise.\n- Corpus-wide check once the SLM labels everything.",
			"x": 6320,
			"y": 10942,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 260,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8 · Disagreement check and answer rule (20 Nov)\n**Why:** decide when the agent must ask (several cases fit) and make sure the final answer holds the right case. Measured on the 83 dev-contradictions questions (step 7), with the right thread always among the documents.\n- Code: exploration/slack_extraction/agreement.py, answer_check.py\n- Todo: [[Show the agent where its results disagree]]",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 13124,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 400,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s8a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8a · Hand review of the set\n- ✓ 5 labels changed: e.g. a \"consistent\" question had a second thread with another workaround; two \"specific\" details named only a channel that two threads share.\n- ✓ 1 specific question dropped: its added detail gave the answer away.\n- Now 83: 27 consistent, 27 specific, 29 ambiguous (dev_contradictions_review.json).",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 13124,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 330,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s8b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8b · Classifier input: snippet or text?\n**Idea:** Gemini sees the question and the shown documents and says same answer / one case fits / several cases fit (ask). Baseline prompt, ask precision / recall:\n- ✗ 300-char snippet (what the agent sees): 0.89 / 0.59, misses 12 of 29 asks\n- ✓ first 3,000 chars: 0.85 / 0.76 (F1 0.80)\n- 12,000 chars: 0.82 / 0.79, no better",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 13578,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 370,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_con8b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (20 Nov): full documents\nSnippets lose the facts that separate near-twins. Full text scores like 3,000 characters (within noise), and the agent already has the full top 5 in its context: the check is one more turn on a cached prefix (~$0.003).",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 13578,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 230,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s8c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8c · Prompt calibration\nAsk F1 on 3,000 chars:\n- baseline 0.80 / 0.81 (two runs)\n- ✗ v2 (rules for details, iterations) 0.77\n- ✗ v3 (each document's case listed first) 0.80 / 0.72\n- ✗ v4 (each document judged on its own) 0.76\n- Same prompt flips 3–6 decisions between runs (±0.05 F1): no prompt beats the baseline.\n- Wrong or mixed answers: 1–3 of 83 in every run; most missed asks still answer from the right thread (the question was written from it).\n- ~$0.5 a run.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 14096,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 450,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_stop8c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: prompt tuning on this set\nDifferences are within run-to-run noise, and the misses come partly from how the questions were written.\n**Reopen if** we have more questions of this kind, or real users' questions.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 14096,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 230,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_dec8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (20 Nov)\n- Keep the baseline prompt.\n- The agent does the check itself on its final top 5 in full, from the cache, then asks or answers.\n- The agent runs on Gemini Flash (agent_llm.py; DeepSeek before). Check ≈ $0.005, whole pipeline ≈ $0.02 a question (docs/solution_plan.md).",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 14630,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 260,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s8d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 8d · Answer: main case + similar cases, cited\n**Why:** the classifier can't always tell which case the person means, so the answer must hold the right one anyway.\n- Rule: answer from the best-fitting document, mention the other similar ones briefly (their case, how they differ).\n- Citations: [n] after every fact, turned into [doc_id] in code, so the app opens the document on click.\n- Gemini answers, a second call checks where the right answer is.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 15014,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 460,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_res8d",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Result (20 Nov): right answer in 96.4% of responses\n- As main answer 84.3%, only as a mentioned case 12.0% (ambiguous: 27.6%)\n- Right thread cited inline 97.6%; 612 of 623 fact lines cited (rest are headings)\n- Mentions other cases in 90% of answers, often 5 at full length\n- $0.85 a run",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 15014,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 330,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Solved (20 Nov)\nThe ambiguity route is closed. Leftovers go into [[Update the pipeline with the accepted experiments]]: cap mentions at 2, the judge rule, the check inside the Gemini agent.",
			"x": 6320,
			"y": 15014,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 260,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_g0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "# Document growth: keeping search accurate as the corpus grows\nThe corpus (512k company documents today) keeps growing. Any per-document processing (summaries, facts, labels, buckets) must:\n- work for every new document, cheaply;\n- not lower the accuracy for documents already indexed.\n\n**Why it's its own problem:** the LLM-summary route for semantic search was stopped partly because accuracy on older documents can't be guaranteed as new ones arrive (see the semantic search failures map, step 5).",
			"x": 7240,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 578,
			"color": "6"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_n17",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 1 · Bucket the corpus\n**Idea:** facts don't separate near-duplicates well enough. Instead, split the 512k documents into buckets (topic → project → feature), like an index note that links every note with a one-line description. The agent reads the index, picks buckets, and searches only inside them. Scales as long as the buckets are good.\n\n**Example (qst_0178):** the topic (quantization) covers 108k docs; the feature name (\"precision annealing\") narrows it to 7.",
			"x": 7240,
			"y": 718,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 400,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_n18",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 1a · Paper: TnT-LLM (Microsoft, 2024)\n[arXiv 2403.12173](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12173)\n\n**What it does:** an LLM summarizes every sampled item, builds a taxonomy from the summaries in many batches, then labels data; a small classifier trained on those labels sorts the rest.\n\n- ✓ Same pattern as our idea: a big model builds the taxonomy, a small model assigns at scale.\n- ✗ Needs an LLM summary per item and taxonomy passes over a large share of the data: far too many tokens for us.",
			"x": 7840,
			"y": 718,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 430,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_n19",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Decision (9 Nov)\nDon't use TnT-LLM as is. Keep its pattern, replace the expensive steps:\n- **Cluster** the gte vectors first (free); the LLM only **names ~2,000 clusters** from ~10 titles each (≈ 4M tokens, ≈ $3).\n- **Titles** instead of LLM summaries (summaries stopped, see step 5 of the semantic search failures map).\n- Taxonomy from a **small sample**; a small model or vector similarity assigns the rest (TopicGPT: open models assign well, but create taxonomies poorly).",
			"x": 8440,
			"y": 718,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 400,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_n20",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next\n**Free check:** cluster the corpus and see whether each of the 46 failures' right document sits in a small cluster with its look-alikes (e.g. qst_0178 in a \"precision annealing\" cluster, not among 108k quantization docs).\n\n**Watch:** when new documents arrive, the buckets must stay accurate for the old ones. A fixed taxonomy lowers this risk but doesn't remove it, so test adding documents too.",
			"x": 9040,
			"y": 718,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 420,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_end6a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: TnT-LLM end to end\nPer-item LLM summaries plus repeated taxonomy passes cost too many tokens on 512k documents.\n\n**Reopen if** LLM tokens get much cheaper.",
			"x": 8440,
			"y": 1198,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_g2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 2 · Keyword routing (9 Nov)\n**Idea:** no clustering; a small model labels the data.\n1. The SLM writes keywords for each document (feature, project, concept; not names or ids).\n2. An LLM deduplicates them into one canonical list, with a mapping.\n3. Store each keyword, its embedding and its documents.\n4. Search the keywords by meaning; they **route** to a small group, and the reranker ranks inside it.",
			"x": 7240,
			"y": 1588,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 424,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_g2a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Why it fits growth\n- A new document only gets keywords from the SLM and is mapped onto the existing list. Old documents are never re-labelled.\n- Two keywords found to be the same later → one row in the mapping.\n- Small: tens of thousands of keyword vectors, not millions.",
			"x": 7840,
			"y": 1588,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 298,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "dg_g2b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Watch / next\n- Keywords must be **feature-level**: a topic like \"quantization\" (108k docs) routes nowhere.\n- Very common keywords and names are noise.\n- The question must be turned into keyword phrases first.\n- **Free test:** with the DeepSeek entities of the 46 failures' candidates, does the question reach a small group that holds the right document?",
			"x": 8440,
			"y": 1588,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 328,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "ab_p0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "# The LLM answers when it should say \"I don't know\"\nWhen search misses the right document, the system should reply \"I could not find this information\". Our agents often answer from a near-duplicate instead.\n\nFound on 9 Nov 2026 while testing clarifying questions on the 46 semantic failures. Not solved yet: a problem for later.",
			"x": 9960,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 392,
			"color": "6"
		},
		{
			"id": "ab_s1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 1 · Measure abstention when search misses\n**Why:** how often does the LLM admit it has no answer when the right document isn't among the documents it retrieved? Semantic questions, Gemini judge.\n- ✓ **Pipeline** (one search, run 15): right doc missed in 56 → **80% \"could not find\"**, 20% answered anyway.\n- ✗ **Snippet agent** (run 18): missed in 49 → only **39% \"could not find\"**, **59% answered anyway** (wrong).\n- ✗ Same on the 46 failures (runs 28–30): 43–51% \"could not find\", 49–58% answered anyway.\n- When the right doc *was* found, almost every answer is correct (68 of 69, 75 of 76).",
			"x": 9960,
			"y": 532,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 548,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "ab_s1c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Conclusions (9 Nov)\n- The agents' extra searches rescue a few questions (semantic 52.8% → 60.8%), but where they don't, the agent is **more likely to answer wrongly than to say it doesn't know**.\n- The pipeline's 11 \"answered anyway\" are mostly ambiguous questions: article 004 found 10 cite a document that does answer. True hallucination ≈ 1 in 56.\n- For the agents, the split between \"a valid other document\" and \"a wrong near-duplicate\" is **not measured yet**.",
			"x": 10560,
			"y": 532,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 408,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "ab_s1n",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ? Next (not started)\n- **Rate the agents' \"answered anyway\" answers** like article 004 (does the cited document answer?): ~$1 Gemini. Tells real hallucinations from ambiguous questions.\n- Ideas to test: a **verify step** (does the chosen document really answer *this* question, or a near-duplicate?), a stricter \"answer only if…\" prompt, scoring abstention in the evaluation (a wrong answer worse than \"I don't know\").\n- Watch: clarifying questions and a source filter may change this rate.",
			"x": 11160,
			"y": 532,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 408,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 4 · Topic modelling (16 Nov)\n**Why:** see what groups the corpus forms by itself, before we fix the schema, and whether the near-twin families share a cluster.\n**Fits \"no second pass\":** the fine-tuned gte vectors of all 512k documents already exist, so clustering them needs no new pass over the text.\nCandidates to look at:\n- **BERTopic:** our vectors → UMAP → HDBSCAN → c-TF-IDF keywords per topic; hierarchical topics; an LLM can name the topics (one call per topic, not per document).\n- **Top2Vec, FASTopic:** other embedding-based topic models.\n- **LDA / NMF:** cheap bag-of-words baselines.\n- Ready models: pretrained BERTopic models, zero-shot topic classifiers.\n- Link: the free cluster check planned in the document growth map (do the 46 failures sit in small clusters?).",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 4178,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 654,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_end4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ⏸ Stopped: topic modelling and clustering (16 Nov)\n- ✗ Topic groups would route to the topic search already finds; the failures are inside a topic, among near-twins.\n- ✗ Clustering adds little over what we have: cosine similarity over the fine-tuned gte vectors already finds a document's neighbours (DiskANN index). Coarse groups only blur the details that separate the twins (MODE's own limitation: it can miss the single best chunk).\n- Topic-RAG and MODE were tested on one domain and up to 500 chunks, not 512k documents from one template.\n**Reopen if** the agent needs a browsable menu of areas (e.g. for broad \"high level\" questions), or on real company data, where documents don't arrive in neat template families.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 4178,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 596,
			"color": "2"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s7",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 7 · Dev-contradictions set (18 Nov)\n**Why:** to calibrate when the agent should ask, we need questions with a known answer to \"do the top results disagree?\". Built from the 200 Slack dev questions and the documents both setups showed (right thread + final top 5 of today's search and of agent v3 top 100).\n- Code: `exploration/slack_extraction/contradictions.py` → `data/dev_contradictions.jsonl`.",
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 11630,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 370,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s7a",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 7a · Compare answers side by side\n**Why:** the ambiguity judge rated each document alone. Two \"full\" documents can be the same case (no need to ask) or different cases (ask). Gemini compared each answering document's answer + quote with the right thread's (189 questions, 772 answers):\n- same case, consistent: 87\n- same case, conflicting facts: 159\n- different case: 526\n- ✗ Real conflicts: 135 of 189 questions (71%), not 76.5%.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 11630,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 420,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s7b",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 7b · Classes\n- **Consistent (28):** every other answering document agrees with the right thread, or none answers.\n- **Ambiguous (135):** another document answers fully with a different case or conflicting facts. 28 kept.\n- Excluded (26): conflicts only with partial answers.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 12110,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 300,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s7c",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## 7c · Specific questions, created and verified\n**Why:** the dev questions are vague on purpose, so none names the detail that picks the right thread.\n- Gemini adds one detail the person would know (channel, customer, team, environment, time); no answer, ids, numbers or names.\n- ✓ Kept only if the right thread still answers fully and every conflicting document now rates \"none\"; up to 3 attempts.\n- ✓ 56 of 107 verified; 28 used.\n- ✗ First pass let a ticket id through: details with digits now rejected.",
			"x": 5120,
			"y": 12470,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 490,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_res7",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ Result (18 Nov): 84 questions, 28 per class\nconsistent / specific / ambiguous, each row with the right answer and the conflicting and agreeing documents.\n**Limits:** only twins the two setups showed; answers compared, not full documents; Gemini labels.\n**Hand review (20 Nov):** 5 labels changed, 1 dropped → 83 questions (27 / 27 / 29), see 8a.",
			"x": 5720,
			"y": 12470,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 300,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_next7",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "### ✓ In use (20 Nov)\nHand-reviewed and used to calibrate the agreement classifier and the answer rule: step 8.",
			"x": 6320,
			"y": 12470,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 200,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s9",
			"type": "text",
			"color": "5",
			"width": 520,
			"height": 430,
			"x": 4520,
			"y": 16028
		},
		{
			"id": "d_s9a",
			"type": "text",
			"color": "5",
